‎## 4. Operating Principle — Rule #15 Candidate
‎
‎**Rule #15: Adaptability as Operating Capability**
‎
‎> Adaptability is not a personality trait. It is operating capability: preserving objective while changing approach when reality changes. Forest changes, ground shifts, crossroads appear, clock doesn't stop. Adaptability = capacity to change response without abandoning underlying purpose. Don't throw away invariant because surface changes. Adapt method, not mission. Observe invariant. Adapt to surface. Don't become attached to road. Become capable of navigating terrain. Road can disappear. Ability to navigate is what travels.
‎
‎**Test for adaptability:**
‎
‎When reality changes (new model, new limit, new pricing, new forest), ask:
‎
‎1. What is my underlying purpose / objective? (invariant)
‎2. What is my current approach / road? (surface)
‎3. Does reality change require changing approach while preserving objective?
‎4. Am I attached to road or capable of navigating terrain?
‎
‎**If attached to road:** "But we always did it this way" / "But my workflow depends on this exact tool" / "But I learned this language" → borrowed ground failure
‎
‎**If capable of navigating terrain:** "Objective remains — what's new approach that preserves it?" → portability, mechanism thinking
‎
‎**Connects to earlier chain:**
‎
‎- Limits → Performance → Portability → Choice → Preservation (Dispatch 018)
‎- Preservation asks: Which capability must survive even if ecosystem changes? (Dispatch 017)
‎- Adaptability answers: The ability to navigate, not the road itself.
‎
